// Constraint layout — Cassowary-style solver (Tower c28/ca3ynsl,
// D-LAYOUT1=A / D-LAYOUT-GATES1=A / D-LAYOUT-CTOR1). `name :: Layout.{ … }`
// is a D-DOTCTOR3 typed literal whose body is `Constraint` elements (GATE 1
// blesses `>=`/`<=`/`==` to return a `Constraint` instead of `Bool`, closed
// to this type family only — same category as D-SIMD2). `box.anchor` reads
// (`left`/`right`/`width`/`top`/`bottom`/`height`) auto-vivify a box scoped
// to the layout; `self.anchor` is the container box named after the binding.
// `left`/`right`/`width` are horizontal (`HVar`), `top`/`bottom`/`height`
// are vertical (`VVar`) — mixing axes is a compile error (E2932).
//
// A two-column form: the label column has a minimum width, the input column
// fills the rest of the row, and a fixed gap separates them. All three
// constraints are REQUIRED (the default); the layout is solved once, then
// read back with `.value()`.
fn run() {
    form :: Layout.{
        label.width >= 80.0
        label.right + 16.0 == input.left
        label.width + 16.0 + input.width == self.width
    }
    // `self.width` inside the literal vivifies the container box
    // (`form.h("form", "width")`) — set it via a soft suggestion (an edit
    // variable): required constraints always win over a suggestion, but with
    // nothing else pinning label.width, the solver settles at its tightest
    // required bound (80.0) and gives the rest of the row to input.width.
    form.suggest(form.h("form", "width"), 300.0)
    label_w :: form.value(form.h("label", "width"))
    input_w :: form.value(form.h("input", "width"))
    print("label width: {label_w}")
    print("input width: {input_w}")
    print("feasible: {(form.is_feasible())}")
}
